#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063840 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/id #uuid "fe39d0a2-20cd-5d77-8801-c1a8575706e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10876" :question/problem "Calculate $F(\\pi)$ where $\\displaystyle F(x) = \\int_1^x \\dfrac 1 t\\,\\textrm d t.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\pi,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\!\\left(\\pi\\right) = \\int_{1}^{\\pi} \\dfrac{1}{t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\pi ) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|\\,{|}_{1}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063841 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "426cd4bd-909d-527f-a35c-9bb10ef3e734" :question/math-academy-id "e-10877" :question/problem "Suppose that a pool has a small hole and that water is leaking out at a rate of $t^{1/3}$ gallons per minute, where $t$ is the time in minutes. If at time $t = 0$ the pool contains $100$ gallons of water, how many gallons of water will remain in the pool after $t$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t) = -t^{1/3}$ be the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing, and let $F(T)$ be the amount of water in the pool after $T$ minutes. Note that $f(t)$ is negative to indicate that the volume is decreasing.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T) \t\t\t= F(0) + \\int_0^T f(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(T) & =F(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100-{\\int }_{0}^{T}{t}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3{t}^{4/3}}{4}{|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3(T)^{4/3}}{4}+\\frac{3(0)^{4/3}}{4} \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3}{4}{T}^{4/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nF(t) = 100 - \\dfrac {3}{4}t^{4/3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063842 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "834e0019-f098-5b33-8579-2fae3f46ba78" :question/math-academy-id "e-10884" :question/problem "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Given $\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{-2}^{t} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/7eb2c5d91fde9279e2533ff77d86ea30e9c21c3a026d26e84e4d04293da6f63a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/8bf473cbddef213fb959112fb72ca9a13d9aae4d72c64ce5a68110f349e34d33.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x \\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_1.$\n\n- $x \\in [0,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_2.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x \\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area $A_1$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x \\in [0,3],$ we compute the area $A_2$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+9=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee816e61-64a8-5b72-a561-38bf9e8f40dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289439} {:answer/id #uuid "f32844d7-93c9-530e-8615-f21d082247b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289440} {:answer/id #uuid "b10c74bc-21b6-56b3-a04b-cc464e4be32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289441} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8d0ab7-1852-521f-94ab-b838c010d72c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289442} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cf20e6-65e4-5ce0-ba21-466f62593d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3cf20e6-65e4-5ce0-ba21-466f62593d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289443} :answer-field/id #uuid "3694a628-218c-5e32-a6d8-a475f9367072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86168165-44dd-55ce-89eb-7dc3a8b70b80" :question/math-academy-id "q-103770" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/749bafb0b977dc67bbf444edc6c51c884a55874655553671330462037c7f1e45.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/813a6e18ee953306699ffe65b5af99c9d44baa1d9e958572a6aad14b4e139f2c.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-1,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a square. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-1,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of the square as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+(4-\\pi )=10-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9818ae35-c8b3-5f88-ba2b-5d45142a8bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23+9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299254} {:answer/id #uuid "73906657-1425-51ce-a538-2aa005fbfb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36+9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299255} {:answer/id #uuid "47f2bfd2-8877-5c8d-baeb-95219fbfd2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18+9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299256} {:answer/id #uuid "83b79d39-0888-504f-8c23-2da1887a9f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45+18\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299257} {:answer/id #uuid "87333285-38e3-5507-b35f-2d193c3ba5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87333285-38e3-5507-b35f-2d193c3ba5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299258} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1e47631-c32b-5c32-9d8a-459bc3b07a5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2db2de8c-b623-527a-a241-ca9def43988b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103771" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/68786f08e73998de597d0d9eea5be3031f322785c05835cf7eccfa7837f36d7b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $3.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(6).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=6,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/b553755a624031408b8374d06c8dc8dbf33c3ddc8d372332c945bad7e42ef068.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,6],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,6],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+(\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18)=\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "587c7f4f-115a-5963-87dd-232b32e687dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305966} {:answer/id #uuid "01d3407a-3c7d-533c-828b-7aa8688d01e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305967} {:answer/id #uuid "beeea8a6-2b47-5b42-b05a-5d0382c3f671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305968} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b79f68-f632-52f0-9e66-51075c7a3df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305969} {:answer/id #uuid "54dddf8a-4af2-50c5-b3f6-f0d58e59b2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01d3407a-3c7d-533c-828b-7aa8688d01e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305967} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0eab8f0-b73b-5d30-8937-5c0f795dbb24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b66c8b3-85fc-5f66-99e3-c1db513f99ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-103772" :question/problem "Calculate $F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =-0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8a8d6e-233e-5b48-8976-c06358afb546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "73\\,225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299261} {:answer/id #uuid "e39d5695-9efa-5e4a-ab6b-7ea5f9ffed29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69\\,270" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299262} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdb847b-1d92-5ccb-ba6b-56b98999ccef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89\\,850" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299263} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb86ed1-24f7-56df-a21e-7d7d67c11dde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\,250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299264} {:answer/id #uuid "3637a9d0-2e3a-5d25-a194-e01b7625e8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83\\,350" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3637a9d0-2e3a-5d25-a194-e01b7625e8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83\\,350" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299265} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ddf580e-bb8e-58b9-8619-130ffdcec2ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8de63944-9034-5911-a181-23fee9b4f403" :question/math-academy-id "q-103776" :question/problem "The bacteria culture in a lab increases at a rate of ${e}^{2t}$ per hour. If, after $2$ hours, there are $2\\,000$ bacteria in the population, approximately how many bacteria are in the population after $6$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "Let $s(t)={e}^{2t}$ be the rate at which the number of bacteria is changing and let $S(T)$ be the total number of bacteria and in the population after $T$ hours.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nS(T)=S(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(T) & =S(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\,000+{\\int }_{2}^{T}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}{|}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2T}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2T}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of bacteria in the culture after $T=6$ hours, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nS(6)=2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\cdot 6}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}=2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{12}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}≈83\\,350.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1846340a-f094-5b83-97a0-f3ac25fc7ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305973} {:answer/id #uuid "50ea147b-8c29-5264-b202-c80fcb3a36f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305974} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e9bce0-a1df-5b6c-94ce-218660ebc8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305975} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2ec7dd-47ce-5fc7-8d89-146d8000e975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305976} {:answer/id #uuid "57db17e7-77e1-5f6b-9b42-dadcd888e7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50ea147b-8c29-5264-b202-c80fcb3a36f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305974} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4aaa94f-e585-5030-b83f-e00cc349746c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65f31595-eae0-5d16-93f8-31fb7f4c123c" :question/math-academy-id "q-103777" :question/problem "The weight, in pounds, of a baby gorilla $t$ years after birth grows at a rate of $3{t}^{2}+15.$ If, at $t=1$ years old, the gorilla weighed $20$ pounds, how much will the gorilla weigh at $3$ years old?" :question/worked-solution "Let $s(t)=3{t}^{2}+15$ be the rate at which the weight of the gorilla is changing and let $S(T)$ be the weight of the gorilla $T$ years after birth. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nS(T)=S(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(T) & =S(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =20+{\\int }_{1}^{T}3{t}^{2}+15\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =20+({t}^{3}+15t){|}_{1}^{T} \\\\  & =20+({T}^{3}+15T)-({1}^{3}+15\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =20+{T}^{3}+15T-16 \\\\  & ={T}^{3}+15T+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the weight of the gorilla $T=3$ years after birth, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nS(3)={3}^{3}+15\\cdot 3+4=76.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1adcfd6c-b637-558e-a7e5-45b52d0ecf31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289453} {:answer/id #uuid "b6045a1b-47db-57e3-9dbb-761ca94a3938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289454} {:answer/id #uuid "e82c20a1-3437-5fdd-aa50-081bba41e3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289455} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4fe3f6-a5aa-57e8-a2b8-6b502c1458f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289456} {:answer/id #uuid "59cae77d-d8dd-5f83-ae0a-319dcffbe847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e82c20a1-3437-5fdd-aa50-081bba41e3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289455} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c722781-d362-5cea-ac9a-80896902ad60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74194f45-8c0e-5305-bf6c-f0844fbf03f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-156193" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/3676c09e0fa21bbf0a0d1828fbb6e6095f83965ea3edd0538786ca11d2fb317e.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/6dad371f36d3d5cbb88bbed40b2e3b60f94fbcad8284b0e218093c13c57d6e41.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+2=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f6d0024-6f94-59d2-9d95-db3a33396651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6d0024-6f94-59d2-9d95-db3a33396651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305980} :answer-field/id #uuid "66111a4a-9f52-5711-ba2a-a4926641bb8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95fcafd6-ede3-5773-a2d9-1d9c45360b25" :question/math-academy-id "q-316149" :question/problem "Calculate $F(4)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$F(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=4,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(4)={\\int }_{0}^{4}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nNow, computing the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b0b70a4-ae66-5361-891c-346719466de2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b0b70a4-ae66-5361-891c-346719466de2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305983} :answer-field/id #uuid "baa852d6-b585-5d61-b0ea-05435134fe04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acf1d4b0-f065-5673-ab59-91c8a1ee5cf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-346530" :question/problem "Calculate $F(2)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$F(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(2)={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nNow, computing the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-(\\frac{{0}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d72ef5ab-f7ac-5d9b-b6bc-4129c414e95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^3-5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d72ef5ab-f7ac-5d9b-b6bc-4129c414e95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^3-5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305986} :answer-field/id #uuid "15c32acc-8411-57aa-bf4c-1b9f705f6c4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "604511ec-52be-5371-a34e-373cb41383f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-346545" :question/problem "Calculate $F(1)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}10{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=1,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(1)={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2}{e}^{2t+1}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =5({e}^{2(1)+1}-{e}^{2(0)+1}) \\\\  & =5({e}^{3}-e).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a1ff148-3e6a-5111-84da-4c4ed26d0f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305989} {:answer/id #uuid "594e318c-686f-50a4-9059-3f3a6e541f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a1ff148-3e6a-5111-84da-4c4ed26d0f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305989} :answer-field/id #uuid "d12f264d-3ba4-5d07-b040-b7466bb9ce84" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "253241dd-52ed-52fc-88ad-d3918465a0e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-346555" :question/problem "Calculate $F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e3c40c-6dd5-54ac-9da9-191b271414e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15+\\frac{3}{2}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e3c40c-6dd5-54ac-9da9-191b271414e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15+\\frac{3}{2}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305993} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d5ec317-c86e-516a-a600-bba4f7067c61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a02ce0b-fcf5-537f-9e67-1e008d4dbc2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-346571" :question/problem "Thermal energy is being added to a device at a rate of $3t$ joules per second. If at time $t=0$ the device already contains $15$ joules of energy, write an expression for $E(t),$ the total energy in the device at time $t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\n$E(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=3t$ represent the rate at which energy enters the device, and let $E(T)$ be the total energy in the device after $T$ seconds.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nE(T)=E(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(T) & =E(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15+{\\int }_{0}^{T}3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={15+(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =15+\\frac{3{T}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nE(t)=15+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08cb67e9-a358-5faf-ab35-30f2db224bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250+4t+\\frac{3}{4}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08cb67e9-a358-5faf-ab35-30f2db224bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250+4t+\\frac{3}{4}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305996} :answer-field/id #uuid "348d0af4-b554-5d1b-b108-66ffbcb3f4b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9296910b-1e10-5a6e-946e-6ace46181e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-346580" :question/problem "In $2010$, a group of biologists started tracking the population of wolves in a mountain reserve. The initial population was $250,$ and the birth and death rates are given by $6+2t$ and $2+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of years since $2010.$  \n\nFind an expression for the population $P(t)$ of wolves in the reserve at any time $t>0.$\n\n$P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change of the population. According to the given information,\n\n$$\np(t)={{6+2t}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population increase}}-{{(2+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population decrease}}=4+1.5t.\n$$\n\nLet $P(T)$ represent the population of wolves $T$ years after $2010.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(4+1.5t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =250+(4t+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =250+4T+\\frac{3}{4}{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nP(t)=250+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}+4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0aa268c-325a-583c-9ba7-0b3d9e89dbc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6+\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305999} {:answer/id #uuid "17e8967b-bca2-5ead-bd67-e3a605574f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306000} {:answer/id #uuid "cd01c205-d891-541a-9c26-db04fe997332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306001} {:answer/id #uuid "cdbee9a5-9083-5d57-8b27-888f5f2b78a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306002} {:answer/id #uuid "ff31466e-2715-58b5-8a54-80bcd839e815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17e8967b-bca2-5ead-bd67-e3a605574f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306000} :answer-field/id #uuid "17d607bb-eab4-51c5-bd28-49c8d0f4c11d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "358026c7-0281-5a08-b21e-c3e38d8526e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-49220" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/e0fc07ac8466381b668877e25cd0c9d8d5258a9061548e7b8efb12f75f6a0f04.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/923b60ed63551bda207f7e3eeb09e62f8b8eb7465a488858bef54bc36ab20baa.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-2,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-2,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89a5ad42-9dca-58fb-9a9a-e8012c27d0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306006} {:answer/id #uuid "05954b36-9879-5aa5-b8ad-313646365495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306007} {:answer/id #uuid "2cff2f70-6a4f-55b2-a55b-ff074a261b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306008} {:answer/id #uuid "8e24b9f8-0720-5cee-9c6e-52a138635e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306009} {:answer/id #uuid "774392c5-e735-59ba-a93b-0a46b703ef79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e24b9f8-0720-5cee-9c6e-52a138635e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306009} :answer-field/id #uuid "a343680e-35d0-5efd-acb6-a8619246c610" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf4d266f-0a91-53b9-91c1-64b2378d6063" :question/math-academy-id "q-49307" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/edcecf885aaf18a3aa6f6726c0485b1885e37132e73c8964de2576f7da5d61bf.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(0).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=0,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040150/assets/bc8905f82a9f7525d6c59df337138e9f3b0417a45bc40f4b76fee2ddedac2eab.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,-2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a square. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,-2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the square as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=1+(6-\\pi )=7-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de1badbd-5bff-539e-9133-3f907f666417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=6t+\\frac{1}{3}(t-2)^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306013} {:answer/id #uuid "7780cd54-6ca4-5d8a-8b64-8af5cfb677d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=9t-\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)^{5/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306014} {:answer/id #uuid "69173b6a-f9ee-5ca2-bafd-167452f321c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=6+3t-\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306015} {:answer/id #uuid "9c04e6cc-f466-5c34-aae8-c335a78f23bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=3t-\\frac{1}{3}(t-2)^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306016} {:answer/id #uuid "891ff7b3-afa6-5615-b4d6-f385869e33a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=6t+\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69173b6a-f9ee-5ca2-bafd-167452f321c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(t)=6+3t-\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306015} :answer-field/id #uuid "f453d496-09d8-518c-a100-b1c1996a6c65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b7ddfb0-8dec-565d-af61-774b8be297c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-90779" :question/problem "A paddling pool contains $6$ gallons of water initially. At $t=0,$ water is pumped into the pool at a constant rate of $3$ gallons per minute. After $t=2$ minutes, water starts leaking from the pool at a rate of $\\sqrt{t-2}$ gallons per minute. How many gallons of water are in the pool at time $t$, for $t\\ge 2?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=3-\\sqrt{t-2}$ be the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing for $t\\ge 2.$ Note that we subtract $\\sqrt{t-2}$ to indicate the rate at which the volume is decreasing.\n\nLet $F(T)$ be the volume of the pool after $T$ minutes. Using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T)=F(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the pool contains $6+2\\cdot 3=12$ gallons of water. Substituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(T) & =F(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{\\int }_{2}^{T}(3-\\sqrt{t-2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{(3t-\\frac{2}{3}{(t-2)}^{3/2})|}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =12+3T-\\frac{2}{3}(T-2)^{3/2}-(3(2)-\\frac{2}{3}(2-2)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =6+3T-\\frac{2}{3}(T-2)^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of our original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nF(t)=6+3t-\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)^{3/2},\\,t\\ge 2.\n$$"}]}
